How to build/run
----------------
cd P4
make
./chatd <port>

To test manually:
./test_client localhost <port>

Files
-----
chatd.c - main server file. sets up the listening socket, handles poll loop, accepts clients, and processes incoming data.
chatd.h - shared definitions, constants, structs, and function declarations used across all files.
protocol.c - parses incoming protocol messages, validates format, and handles NAM, SET, MSG, WHO, and ERR responses.
clientlist.c - maintains connected clients, including adding/removing clients and searching by file descriptor or name.
utils.c - helper functions such as write_all and validation functions for names and message text.
test_client.c - write here
Makefile - builds the chatd executable.
README - README file.
AUTHOR - AUTHOR file with NetIDs.

Program design
--------------
Server model:
The server uses a single-threaded design with poll() to handle multiple clients concurrently.
Each connected client is represented by a file descriptor and stored in a fixed-size array.

Main loop:
The server runs an infinite loop:
poll() waits for activity
accept() handles new connections
read() processes incoming messages
disconnected clients are removed

Protocol format:
All messages follow the format:
version|type|length|fields...|

Version:
"1" is required

Types:
NAM - set username
SET - set status
MSG - send message
WHO - query users
ERR - error message

The length field specifies the exact number of bytes in the message body.

Client structure:
Each client stores:
file descriptor
named flag (whether NAM has been completed)
username (max 32 chars)
status (max 64 chars)

Message handling:
NAM:
validates name length and allowed characters
ensures name is unique
marks client as named

SET:
updates client status
broadcasts status change to all clients

MSG:
broadcasts to all users if recipient is "#all"
sends private message if recipient is a specific user

WHO:
returns list of all users for "#all"
returns single user's status if specific name is requested

Error handling:
ERR 0 - malformed or unreadable message (connection closed)
ERR 1 - name already in use
ERR 2 - unknown recipient
ERR 3 - illegal character
ERR 4 - too long
ERR 5 - invalid state (e.g., MSG or WHO before NAM)

Validation:
Names must be 1–32 characters
Allowed characters: letters, digits, '-' and '_'

Status and messages:
Characters must be ASCII 32–126
Status max length: 64
Message max length: 80

Testing
-------
Basic functionality:
NAM with valid and invalid names
SET with valid status
WHO #all and WHO <user>
MSG to #all and private MSG

Error testing:
Malformed messages (missing fields, incorrect length)
Duplicate names
Illegal characters in names
Unknown recipients
Commands before NAM

Multi-client testing:
Multiple clients connected simultaneously using test_client
Verified broadcast messages appear on all clients
Verified private messages only reach intended recipient

test_client testing:
write here

Edge cases:
Empty input
Long names and messages
Client disconnects
Invalid protocol formatting

Notes
-----
The server assumes one complete message per read() call.
Partial TCP message handling is not implemented.

